# DEBT-0009 — Constitutional Amendment Hard-Loop Implemented in Runtime (Z2) Without Kernel (Z0/Z1) Gate or Ledger Persistence

**Status**: OPEN  
**Filed**: 2026-07-30  
**Priority**: Medium  
**Category**: Architectural Deviation / Layering Breach  

---

## 1. Compromise
The constitutional amendment hard-loop process (`AmendmentPath`, `prepare_amendment`) is implemented inside [runtime/amendment.py](file:///Users/bob/GitHub/paeos/runtime/amendment.py) in Trust Zone 2 (Z2 runtime). Furthermore, `prepare_amendment` returns an in-memory `RatificationPacket` object without recording an `amendment_proposed` or `adversary_review_completed` event to the single-writer kernel ledger.

## 2. Reason
During Phase 2 (B2.E), the amendment preparation pathway needed to compose `InformationBarrierManager`, `ReviewHarness`, `AgentDispatcher`, and `ClaudeCodeRuntime` to execute the adversarial attack against proposed safety-invariant diffs. Because these review components were built in `runtime/`, the orchestrating module was placed in `runtime/amendment.py`.

## 3. Ideal Solution
Move amendment path enforcement into the kernel TCB (`kernel/amendment.py` or a dedicated kernel gate). Every amendment proposal, classification check, and adversarial review completion MUST emit a signed event to the single-writer kernel ledger, ensuring durable record keeping per PAEOS-0 Axiom A2 ("artifacts durable, context ephemeral").

## 4. Repayment Conditions
Repay during Phase 3 TCB hardening prior to enabling autonomous hard-loop execution.

## 5. Estimated Impact
Medium. If un-repaid, transient amendment preparation traces could be lost on process failure, and untrusted Z2 runtime code could bypass TCB monitoring during hard-loop proposal preparation.
